 # Regularization losses
 
+Also called: Regularization terms, Auxiliary losses, Priors
 
 ## Overview
-Regularization losses are auxiliary objective terms added to the total optimization function in differentiable volume rendering to suppress artifacts, enforce physical plausibility, and prevent overfitting. Unlike photometric losses that measure reconstruction fidelity against input images, these losses operate on scene parameters, geometric properties, or statistical distributions [S1, S2, S4, unofficial]. They set themselves apart from sibling methods like [Optimization Loops and Training Procedures](5622-optimization-loops-and-training-procedures.md) by directly modifying the gradient landscape to favor specific structural priors (e.g., smoothness, compactness) rather than just adjusting the frequency of updates.
+Regularization losses are auxiliary objective functions added to the primary photometric reconstruction loss during the optimization of differentiable volume rendering fields. They solve the ill-posed nature of inverse rendering by enforcing physical plausibility, geometric consistency, or statistical priors on the scene parameters (density, geometry, transfer functions) or the rendered output, thereby preventing overfitting, suppressing artifacts like "floaters," and improving perceptual quality [S1, unofficial]. This family is distinguished from sibling methods like [Data Consistency Loss](2976-data-consistency-loss.md) (which measures fidelity to input data) and [Optimization Loops](5622-optimization-loops-and-training-procedures.md) (which manage training dynamics) by operating directly on the latent model structure or derived geometric quantities rather than pixel-wise color errors [S1, unofficial].
 
